joinly v0.5.4 files, changed by switchboard (#713, #763, #820, #824, DESIGN.md sections 52.5 and 52.9)

server.py and kokoro.py are copies of two files of joinly v0.5.4
(https://github.com/joinly-ai/joinly, tag v0.5.4), which is MIT licensed:
Copyright (c) 2025 Dominik Brockmann, Johannes Dittrich, Fynn Aurand. The licence is
at the top of each file, as it requires. switchboard is not affiliated with joinly.

They are mounted read-only over the image's own copies, pinned by sha256 (docs/DEPLOY.md,
"A meeting bot"), until a joinly release lets `speak_text` take a voice.

What changed from v0.5.4

  joinly/server.py
    - `speak_text` takes an optional `voice` (a Kokoro voice name: a-z, 0-9 and _, 1 to 32
      characters), for that call of the tool only.
    - It sets the voice for the length of the call and clears it afterwards.
    - When Kokoro did not know the voice, the answer reads "Finished speaking. The voice was
      not found, so the session's voice was used." instead of "Finished speaking."
    - Two tools, `share_image` and `stop_share_image` (#763): the first takes the base64 of a PNG or
      JPEG (checked by its bytes, at most 8 MB and 50 million pixels), draws it on a canvas in the Zoom
      page and hands the page's Share button that canvas as the stream, through an override of
      getDisplayMedia that lasts for the share. No URL is taken or opened. If Zoom says the host has not
      allowed sharing, the tool fails with "Host disabled attendee screen sharing.". The second ends it.

  joinly/services/tts/kokoro.py
    - A context variable holds the voice of the line being spoken. `_tts` reads it and falls
      back to the session's voice (`tts_args`) when it is unset or Kokoro has no such voice.

Both files are also formatted with this repository's ruff settings, so its lint passes (line
breaks and an import order only). Without a `voice`, and unless `share_image` is called, joinly behaves as v0.5.4.
The same two files are in plugins/claude/meeting/joinly/ and plugins/codex/meeting/joinly/;
tests/unit/test_joinly_voice_patch.py keeps the three copies identical.

captions.py (#764, DESIGN.md section 52.7) is not a copy of a joinly file: it is switchboard's own, MIT licensed like
the rest of this repository, a new file mounted at joinly/captions.py. The patched server.py above points joinly's
`stt`, `vad` and `transcription_controller` settings at its three classes when asked for `captions`, hands the
controller the meeting provider, and adds the `transcription_status` tool. Only the server's bot mounts it.

The bot's logo (#820, DESIGN.md section 52.10) is in server.py too: `BOT_LOGO_SVG` is switchboard's favicon, and
`_install_bot_logo()` runs when the file is imported: it sets joinly's `camera_feed._LOGO_SVG` to it and wraps each
platform controller's `join` to click the camera button once afterwards (a failure is logged once and never fails
the join). `SWITCHBOARD_BOT_VIDEO=0` turns it off. Nothing else of joinly changes.

The bot's own mute button (#853, DESIGN.md section 52.12) is in server.py too: `mute_status` reads the
accessible names of the buttons on the call page (Zoom "unmute my microphone" / "mute my microphone", Meet
"turn on mic" / "turn off mic", Teams "Unmute" / "Mute") and answers whether the bot's own button says it is
muted. Zoom's and Meet's own names win over another participant's Unmute. It does not click. A host's
"Mute all" is not that button. `unmute_yourself` is joinly's own tool and is unchanged.

The Zoom participant list (#824, DESIGN.md section 52.7) is in server.py too: `_install_zoom_participants()` replaces the
Zoom controller's `get_participants` when the file is imported. joinly's own looks for one button and gives up when it
is not visible at that moment; this one tries every match of the button (then a click made in the page), the "More"
menu's Participants entry (exactly that, never "Mute all participants"), then Alt+U, waits for a slow panel instead of
toggling it closed, reads the visible panel's rows and closes it, and logs one line (counts, visibility and boxes,
never names) when it still fails. Nothing at import can stop the bot: any exception there is logged and joinly's own
method is kept. The mounted `zoom.py` (a commit of a fork, pinned by address) is not changed.
